# OpenScreenTime — production environment for compose.yaml.
#
# deploy/setup.sh --domain ost.example.com writes this for you (with fresh
# secrets). By hand: cp .env.example .env, then set the two marked values.

# ---- The one setting ----------------------------------------------------------
# The public https address your reverse proxy serves (what the browser shows).
# The passkey domain (RP_ID), the origin (RP_ORIGIN) and secure cookies are all
# derived from it.
OST_PUBLIC_URL=https://ost.example.com

# ---- Postgres ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials for the bundled `db` service. Set a long random password; it
# exists nowhere else (deploy/backup.sh copies this file to backups/env.backup).
POSTGRES_USER=openscreentime
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-password
POSTGRES_DB=openscreentime

# DATABASE_URL is derived in compose.yaml from the three vars above. Only set
# it yourself when pointing at an external Postgres.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://openscreentime:change-me@db:5432/openscreentime

# ---- Host port ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The server's port 8080 is published to $OST_BIND_ADDR:$OST_PORT on the HOST
# only — your reverse proxy forwards to it.
OST_PORT=8080

# Which host address to publish on. The default (127.0.0.1) is right when the
# reverse proxy runs on this same machine. If the proxy lives on a DIFFERENT
# host, set this to the LAN/VPN address it can reach — e.g. 192.168.8.131.
# Never 0.0.0.0: the server trusts X-Forwarded-For, so anyone who can reach
# this port directly could hand it any client IP and walk past the rate limiter.
# OST_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# First-run registration gate. deploy/setup.sh generates and prints this; the
# first parent types it once. Unset = open first-run (only for a local checkout).
# OST_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN=

# ---- Updates ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The image deploy/update.sh pulls. Default: the one CI publishes from main.
# Set a version tag to pin, or `build` to always compile on this machine.
# OST_IMAGE=ghcr.io/one-more-refactor/openscreentime:latest

# ---- Overrides you normally don't need ---------------------------------------
# Derived from OST_PUBLIC_URL; set only if they must differ.
# RP_ID=ost.example.com
# RP_ORIGIN=https://ost.example.com
# Cookies are Secure for an https public URL. 1 forces them off (plain-http
# testing only), 0 forces them on.
# OST_INSECURE_COOKIES=1
# Rate limiting keys clients by the last X-Forwarded-For hop (on by default,
# because this stack sits behind a proxy). 0 only without any proxy.
# OST_TRUST_PROXY=1

# ---- OIDC SSO (optional) -----------------------------------------------------
# Leave all three unset to disable OIDC (passkey-only login still works). If
# the provider is unreachable, the SSO button just stays hidden until it is.
# OST_OIDC_ISSUER=https://issuer.example.com
# OST_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=openscreentime
# OST_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me
# OST_OIDC_NAME=SSO

# ---- Phone alerts (optional) -------------------------------------------------
# One-way chat-bot notifications: confirmed tamper / device lockdown, new time
# requests — and server problems (a failed backup or update, the database down,
# a device silent for a day), once per incident. Configure either (or both).
#
# A Discord or Slack "incoming webhook" URL — the easiest option, just paste it:
# OST_ALERT_WEBHOOK=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/xxx/yyy
#
# …or a Telegram bot (message @BotFather to make one), then pair your phone in
# the console (Settings → Security & access → Phone):
# OST_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456:ABC-DEF...

# ---- Logging ------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUST_LOG=openscreentime_server=info,tower_http=info,info
